INT. HOSPITAL SUBBASEMENT - LAUNDRY ROOM - NIGHT

A low, steam-choked cavern. Industrial washers thump like slow drums. Sheet-laden carts line the walls in ghostly rows. Pipes sweat overhead, and the tiled floor is slick with soap and condensation. A single fluorescent tube buzzes.

DR. EZEKIEL FAIRWEATHER, 50s, a cardiothoracic surgeon, sits on an overturned laundry bin in rumpled blue scrubs, his tie shoved in his pocket. Grey stubble. Hands folded between his knees as if praying. He has not slept in thirty hours. He stares at the drain in the floor.

The doors at the far end swing open and a gurney wheel squeals. PADDY GALLAGHER, 50s, the night porter, pushes a cart of towels down the aisle. Barrel-chested, flushed, a green cap pulled over a bald crown. He stops short at the sight of the doctor.

PADDY
Jesus, Mary and Joseph. Doctor?

EZEKIEL
Don't mind me.

PADDY
You scared the life out of me.

EZEKIEL
Sorry.

PADDY
It's the wrong end of the building for you, sir. The surgeons have their own lounge.

EZEKIEL
I know.

PADDY
Then why--

EZEKIEL
It's loud here.

Paddy considers this. He parks the cart. Pulls a crumpled pack of cigarettes from his pocket, glances up at the smoke detector, and puts them back.

PADDY
Bad one?

EZEKIEL
Seven hours. We lost him on the table.

PADDY
I'm sorry.

EZEKIEL
He was thirty-one.

PADDY
Ah, God.

EZEKIEL
He had a daughter. She was in the waiting room, drawing a horse.

PADDY
Did you tell her?

EZEKIEL
His wife did. I just stood there.

A washer ends its cycle with a shuddering groan. Steam breathes out of the vents. Paddy drags a second bin across the tile and lowers himself onto it with a grunt, so the two men sit knee to knee in the damp.

PADDY
My brother went the same way. Heart.

EZEKIEL
When?

PADDY
Twenty years. The doctors were very kind.

EZEKIEL
That doesn't help, does it?

PADDY
Not on the day.

EZEKIEL
PADDY
(speaking at once)
What does, then?
(speaking at once)
Time, I suppose.

They both stop. The ghost of a smile crosses Paddy's face. Ezekiel laughs, one dry exhale through his nose, and rubs his eyes with the heels of his hands.

PADDY (CONT'D)
You want a towel? They're warm.

EZEKIEL
I'm not crying.

PADDY
Never said you were. They're just warm.

He passes a folded towel from the cart. Ezekiel takes it, presses it to his face, and holds it there much longer than a person needs to wipe off sweat. Paddy gazes politely at a washer. Time passes in the slow rhythm of the drums.

EZEKIEL
(muffled)
Thank you.

PADDY
Aye.

EZEKIEL
Does it ever stop? The... carrying it?

PADDY
I wouldn't know, Doctor. I only carry the linen.

EZEKIEL
(lowering the towel)
Then you're luckier than I am.

PADDY
Maybe. Or I just do the washing so you can do the rest.

Ezekiel looks at him properly for the first time. Paddy meets the look without flinching, wide-faced and unhurried.

EZEKIEL
What's your name?

PADDY
Paddy. Paddy Gallagher.

EZEKIEL
Ezekiel.

PADDY
I know. It's on the plaque in the lobby. The one with the donors.

EZEKIEL
I never donated.

PADDY
Your name's on it anyway.

The ghost of a smile returns. Ezekiel stands. He folds the towel into a perfect square and sets it back on the cart, then stands for a moment with his hand on the metal rail.

EZEKIEL
I should go up.

PADDY
They'll keep.

EZEKIEL
Not all of them.

PADDY
No. Not all.

Ezekiel nods. He straightens his scrubs, pulls his tie from his pocket, and puts it back again. At the door he pauses.

EZEKIEL
Paddy.

PADDY
Doctor.

EZEKIEL
Thank you for the towel.

PADDY
Anytime. It's what I'm for.

The door sighs shut behind the surgeon. Paddy sits alone in the thrum of the machines. After a moment he takes out the cigarettes, looks at the smoke detector again, and slowly, decidedly, puts them back.
